<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Magna Graecia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from 8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Great Greece</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Magna Graecia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> for Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, was the name given to the coastal areas of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> settled by Greek colonists from the 8th century BCE. Cities such as Croton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sybaris">Sybaris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a>, Locri and Kaulonia grew rich from farming and trade, produced fine coinage and hosted philosophers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoras">Pythagoras</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> absorbed the region in the 3rd century BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magna-graecia.jpg" alt="Magna Graecia" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bruno Rijsman · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paestum_Temples_(Italy,_October_2020)_-_16_(50562474147).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
